As the result we have a set of k8s manifests that we can deploy to our k8s cluster. Aspirate uses kustomize by default to generate these manifests, so we can use kustomize to deploy them. Here is a generate files structure:

```bash
aspirate-output
├── aspire-discovery-dashboard
│   ├── deployment.yaml
│   ├── kustomization.yaml
│   └── service.yaml
├── dashboard.yaml
├── kustomization.yaml
├── messaging
│   ├── kustomization.yaml
│   ├── service.yaml
│   └── statefulset.yaml
├── namespace.yaml
├── postgres
│   ├── kustomization.yaml
│   ├── service.yaml
│   └── statefulset.yaml
└── redis
    ├── kustomization.yaml
    ├── service.yaml
    └── statefulset.yaml
```

! Important note:

By default aspirate does not generate health checks for the services, so we need to add them manually. Similarly, there is no possiblity to add ingress and load balancer. All these additional resources are not generated by aspirate, so we need to add them manually.

Aspirate allows to generate also helm charts. However, due to the above limitations, it is not recommended to use helm charts for deployment. It would be hard to maintain customizations for the generated helm charts, and it would be hard to maintain the generated k8s manifests as well.